            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your 1988 house is in pretty good shape, however there are a few areas of air leakage (heat loss) that would be addressed in conjunction with completing some of the upgrade recommendations in this report:



** Air leakage through some of the older wood windows



** Some air leakage through the crawlspace walls &amp; the perimeter header space attachment to the main floor above 



** I think the dryer vent is not well sealed (connected) to the outside. Perhaps the old oil tank vent &amp; fill pipes as well



** The intentional holes (5" chimney for furnace, plus the 6" combustion air supply pipe) would be eliminated if the new furnace is a high-efficiency, condensing, direct vent unit



Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in overall air leakage, that could potentially save $ 31 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.3 GJ, and qualify for $ 500 in rebates. These savings might look a bit low, but please know that some of the savings (for removing the chimney &amp; 6" combustion air supply pipe) are associated with the heating system upgrade savings outlined separately in this report. For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior main floor walls of your house are 2x6 @ 16" (R20) and I have modeled the addition of another R10 (eg. 2" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the exterior of the main floor walls.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). Most homeowners participating in this program are already committed to (or seriously considering) an exterior siding + new windows retrofit ............ so our rebate is meant to help cover most, if not all, of the cost of the additional insulation to go behind that new siding.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 300 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose to add 3" (R15), the rebate increases to $5,000.



NOTE: Without an eligible exterior wall insulation upgrade, many of the other rebates in this report are not eligible. This is further outlined in the "Additional Comments" section of this report.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls are uninsulated with 1 layer of semi-rigid fibreglass insulation that is approximately R8, and there is a sheet of poly vapour barrier on the inside that is in poor shape &amp; hanging down in many locations. The header space above has a mixture of insulation products, with most being blue rigid foam board insulation (also about R8).



This crawlspace area is a tough one. You mentioned the house was previously encroaching on the neighbour property and needed to be shifted left and the foundation modified. I don't think the transition from the upper house to the floor header space to the lower concrete walls is well air or vapour sealed. In many of these "tough transition" areas, a lot of builders will use a spray foam product that can provide a continuous transitional layer of insulation R-value, and serve as a good quality air/vapour barrier - achieving 3 goals at once.



The furnace supply ductwork travels through the crawl and there are a few heat supply openings, however many of them are taped shut, but not all ............ so it is indeed heated space. I do not think there is a return air supply from the crawlspace to the furnace and this is likely a good thing as the air down there is musty and you do not want to pull that air through the house.



As discussed, I think you should do the following:

1. Ensure any water that accumulates down there is immediately pumped out (sump pump)

2. Ensure the clothes dryer vent is not pushing hot, damp air down there

3. Adequately insulate, air/vaqpour seal the walls &amp; header space

4. Heat the space to keep the floor above reasonably warm

5. Install a seasonal ventilation system down there where you can use an exhaust fan to draw air through &amp; out of the crawlspace during the non-heating season - air ventilation so it doesn't become musty



I have modeled an additional R10 minimum (ie: R18-R20 total of spay foam) to the walls &amp; header of the crawlspace.



Savings: By adding an additional net R10 of insulation to your crawlspace walls and header space, you could potentially save $ 266 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.8 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 1,000 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade. Having good air quality down there is just as important as the energy savings !!!



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</foundation>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house (except for the small octagon window in the front entrance) appear to be original to the 1988 construction of your house (date taken from the real estate listing you showed me), and the mechanical opening cranks through the wood frames appear worn. They are all however triple pane glass units with warm edge spacers, that appear to be well installed into the walls.



I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all 7 of the current windows (including the front octagon window) with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing all of the above windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 113 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,750 in rebates (7 windows max @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 178 litre electric hot water tank. We discussed an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an Energy Factor of 0.92 or higher.



FYI - in the past 9 years, I have not seen very many electric hot water tanks installed in the nearly 450 new houses built in Yellowknife. Most have on-demand or will use their high efficiency boiler (usually propane) to also heat their domestic hot water year round.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.92 EF, could potentially save $ 1,153 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a relatively new, higher efficiency oil l furnace with a rated efficiency of 86.6%.  For your ability to make an informed decision about whether you want to continue using this furnace or perhaps look at a high efficiency propane alternative, I said that I would model this for you.



I have modeled what the energy &amp; cost savings would be if you had installed an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This furnace option would eliminate the current 5" chimney &amp; the lower 6" combustion air supply in the furnace room. The upper 6" Fresh Air Supply connected to the return air plenum of the furnace (with the integrated control damper) would remain.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 657 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 24.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

