                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $10,250 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the main floor wall insulation

     - $1,000 for the crawlspace wall insulation

     - $3,000 for the heating system

     - $1,750 for 7 new windows

     - $   500 for a 10% air leakage reduction

     - $1,000 for the bonus rebate (if you achieve a minimum 20% reduction it total energy use)



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be for the heating system and crawlspace insulation, however these would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - On-Demand Hot Water (minimum 0.92 EF) - $750

     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the 2 x bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 3.0 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is chimney vented, and could be susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke &amp; carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood fan does not currently vent outside

        - 2 x bathroom fans

        - The clothes dryer (the pressure reading on my blower door was low, &amp; I think it is not fully venting outside)



The above exhaust ventilation will pull in "makeup air" via the 2 x 6" openings in the furnace room. The lower 6" pipe is called a Combustion Air Supply and is intended to allow outside combustion air to enter the furnace room for the furnace to use during combustion. The upper 6" pipe is called a Fresh Air Supply and is directly connected to the return air plenum of the furnace (the damper was in the closed position during my evaluation) - I had emailed you some pictures of this with some advice on how you may want to adjust that damper based upon indoor air quality &amp; moisture conditions. When the Fresh Air Supply damper is partially/fully open, it will draw in some fresh outside air each time the furnace operates (mix it with the internal air entering the return plenum of the furnace - heating the "mixed" air) &amp; distribute it throughout the house, via the heat supply ductwork system.



NOTE: either one of the above 2 x 6" pipes can allow "makeup air" into the house when an exhaust fan operates - so even if the top 6" is closed, air can still come in through the lower 6" Combustion Air Supply pipe. During the blower door test, you could see (via the Infrared Camera images &amp; feeling with your hand) all of the outside cold air coming into the house under that furnace room door. As discussed, those2 x 6" pipes can also work in reverse, sucking warm air out of your house on a cold winter, windy day.



As discussed, you should always ensure adequate "spot ventilation" exists to control sources of excess humidity or odours:



- Venting the range hood to the outside would be a good idea

- You may want to consider installing timer switches for the bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat

- ensure the dryer vent is venting to the outside &amp; not inside the house (main floor or crawlspace)



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



